{"name":"Berry Charm","hex":"#4f4763","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"berry","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small succulent fruit, of any one of many varieties.","A soft fruit which develops from a single ovary and contains seeds not encased in pits."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To pick berries.","To bear or produce berries."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/berry"},{"term":"charm","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An object, act or words believed to have magic power (usually carries a positive connotation).","The ability to persuade, delight or arouse admiration."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To seduce, persuade or fascinate someone or something.","To use a magical charm upon; to subdue, control, or summon by incantation or supernatural influence; to ensorcel or exert a magical effect on."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/charm"}],"entries":[{"title":"Berry","blocks":[{"text":"A berry is a small, pulpy, and often edible fruit. Typically, berries are juicy, rounded, brightly colored, sweet, sour or tart, and do not have a stone or pit although many pips or seeds may be present.‍ Common examples of berries in the culinary sense are strawberries, raspberries, blueberries, blackberries, white currants, blackcurrants, and redcurrants.‍ In Britain, soft fruit is a horticultural term for such fruits.‍"}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Berberis_thunbergii_berries.jpg/330px-Berberis_thunbergii_berries.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berry","wikidata":"Q16128920"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Norwegian_blueberry.jpg/250px-Norwegian_blueberry.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Norwegian_blueberry.jpg/500px-Norwegian_bluebe